@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+# Progressive AI Triage and Agent Budget Architecture
+
+Status: design baseline for `feat/agent-triage-policy-20261021` (2026-10-21).
+
+## Verified baseline
+
+This branch starts from `main` at `4f75a65775b202b8220a208ebdb50fdb5c8dbb4d`. Existing code has explicit scan consent, bounded R1/R2 candidate selection, fixed-commit read-only source tools, source citation validation, cancellation, per-operation request/token limits, and deterministic risk snapshots. R1 is one structured model call per selected candidate; conditional R2 runs a bounded tool Agent. Existing budgets are per candidate/operation, not shared across endpoints.
+
+The new `src/workbench/progressive_triage` package currently supplies strict policy presets and local persistence, conservative deterministic grouping, and evidence-based ranking as pure functions. The settings API and settings tab are connected. **The policy is not frozen into audit consent or consumed by the Worker.** No quick-screen request, shared task ledger, stop state machine, or user-visible group status is live in this phase. `max_cost_usd` is a policy estimate only; Provider bills are not hard-capped. Static-only tasks must continue to make zero model requests.
+
+## Conflict and integration boundary
+
+| Area | Existing owner/work | Integration rule |
+|---|---|---|
+| `operations_api.py`, `operations_store.py`, `operations.py` | #40 multi-mode task DAG, frozen consent and endpoint coordination | Do not change until the current #40 compatible baseline is integrated and reviewed. Add policy snapshot and shared budget through additive migrations and consent-hash changes. |
+| `agent.py`, `smart_audit.py` | #41 cross-file tools, Skeptic, evidence validation, R1/R2 | Do not replace the Agent. Add a separate `quick_screen` contract and bounded call path, then route only authorized selected groups to the existing Agent. |
+| scan forms, consent modal, risk center | #40 authorization and #42 reporting | UI must show exactly what quick screen sends, endpoint and whole-task caps, Provider/model, and soft-cost limitations before consent. |
+| source synchronization/detectors | #39 and detector lanes | Out of scope. Keep fixed source SHA, source inventory, detector versions, and candidate evidence immutable. |
+
+The current feature branch is based on `main`, not the local unmerged follow-up branch. That follow-up has separate work on task DAG/Agent APIs; it must be reconciled by the integration owner before this branch changes shared operation or Agent files. Never copy these new policy files over their branch wholesale.
+
+## State flow
+
+`static_completed -> grouped -> deterministic_ranked -> quick_screening -> deep_investigating -> evidence_judged -> report_ready`
+
+Each group can independently be `not_selected`, `queued`, `running`, `completed`, `partial`, `blocked`, `cancelled`, or `budget_exhausted`. Missing or partial parser/source evidence stays `unknown`; model `possible_refutation` is only a review lead. Scheduler states never change human disposition or `validated_vulnerability`.
+
+### L0: preserve and group
+
+Keep the original report and every candidate ID intact. Build a versioned, content-hashed manifest with reversible member IDs. Exact duplicate grouping requires the same fixed repository/commit/report, source hash, candidate identity, detector/rule, and location. Root-cause grouping additionally requires deterministic source-derived entry, sink, mechanism, relation, and fact identities. Path/CWE/message similarity alone never merges candidates. If fixed source identity is incomplete, keep the observation separate and mark coverage unknown.
+
+### L1: select locally
+
+Compute deterministic score components from verified source binding, parser/coverage state, source-bound security facts, unresolved evidence obligations, change status, impact label, and root-cause evidence. Severity cannot stand in for confidence. Apply family coverage before filling remaining slots by score, with stable ID tie-breaking. The selector only chooses which groups may consume optional model budget; every unselected candidate remains visible as not screened, not safe.
+
+### L2: quick screen (bounded executor implemented; scheduling not yet connected)
+
+The isolated `quick_screen.py` executor builds one strict-schema, no-tools, no-retry request per selected group, with up to four fixed-source evidence excerpts, a 16 KiB excerpt cap, 512 output-token cap, and explicit source/consent/provider/model checks. It validates returned citations against supplied evidence IDs and requires Provider usage. Failures, missing usage, invalid citations, or malformed output become `quick_unknown`; they do not drop the group. `possible_refutation` does not suppress or auto-close it. Static-only authorization is rejected before any Provider call. This executor is not yet persisted or called by a Worker.
+
+### L3: deep Agent (planned)
+
+Send only a quick-screen `investigate` group, or an explicitly recorded deterministic exception, to the existing bounded fixed-commit Agent and Skeptic workflow. Reuse its evidence graph and stop/no-progress guards. Do not duplicate an old R1 call for a group already quick-screened. Keep all results at candidate level until independent human/product validation.
+
+## Policy, consent, and budget data
+
+Current settings are a revisioned private sidecar (`progressive-triage-policy.json`) with a canonical policy SHA and append-only update history. It is deliberately separate from Provider credentials. Existing phase-one API:
+
+- `GET /api/v1/agent/triage-policy`
+- `PUT /api/v1/agent/triage-policy` with optimistic `expected_revision`
+
+Before Worker use, each new task must freeze `policy_version`, `policy_sha256`, selected limits, provider/model and rate-card fingerprint, source scope, and consent hash. A configuration change applies only to new tasks. Expanding source scope or limits requires a new preview and explicit authorization.
+
+The isolated `ledger.py` implements transactional task-wide group dispatch and request reservations in a private SQLite file, keyed by parent audit ID. `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` serializes concurrent reservations. It reserves call/input/output/concurrency/deep-step limits, checks the task elapsed wall deadline, gates deep work on a completed `investigate` quick screen, and returns an explicit idempotent-replay marker so recovery cannot silently resend a possibly billed request. Trusted Provider usage settles actual tokens; missing or ambiguous outcomes retain the full pre-call reservation instead of refunding as zero. Current schema stores task/endpoint/group/stage, reservation key, limits, usage, state and cost estimate. Migration compatibility with a prior deployed ledger schema remains to be verified. For multi-endpoint audits, one parent operation must own this ledger; that integration is not connected yet.
+
+The ledger can enforce local token, model-call, step, concurrency and elapsed-wall-time limits when callers reserve before every request. USD remains advisory: strict-cost mode currently fails closed because no Provider-side cap has been proven. No actual Provider charge or estimate is available from this ledger; it reports USD as unknown.
+
+## Migrations and compatibility
+
+Do not rewrite/delete historical scans, operations, consent, or reports. Use additive SQLite migrations with backups and restart-safe idempotence. Old tasks without policy snapshots keep their existing behavior and are labeled legacy; they are never silently assigned the current policy. Preserve current R0/R1/R2 research records for replay. New progressive records reference the immutable report SHA and source commit; they do not modify the original report.
+
+## API/UI integration sequence
+
+1. Phase 1 (this branch): policy presets/validation, revisioned storage and read/update API, deterministic grouping/ranking, settings tab, 500-observation fixture test.
+2. Phase 2 (partial): bounded quick-screen builder/executor and mock Provider contract tests exist; result persistence and scheduling through the #41 Agent flow remain open.
+3. Phase 3 (partial): isolated parent-task reservation ledger with concurrency, conservative ambiguous-outcome settlement, idempotency, stop reasons and per-stage limits exists; it is not yet connected to the #40 operation DAG/Worker and migration recovery needs integration tests.
+4. Phase 4: bind the frozen policy and consent into snapshot, time-range and version-matrix previews; connect Worker, quick-screen/deep routing, group/usage APIs and risk-center UI. All endpoints share the parent budget.
+5. Phase 5: mock end-to-end and controlled browser tests, then separately authorized real Provider and frozen vulnerable/fixed/safe comparisons. No cost/recall claim before actual measurement.
+
+## Required checks
+
+- Unit: zero, ten, 500, malformed binding, parser unknown, duplicates, family coverage, deterministic rerun, no candidate mutation, policy revision races.
+- Integration: no-consent/static-only zero Provider calls; authorized quick-screen schema and small context; invalid output degrades to unknown; shared multi-endpoint atomic reservation; ambiguous timeout is never replayed/refunded as zero; legacy DB migration reads old tasks.
+- Delivery: Python suite, frontend tests/build, mock API-to-Worker flow, and UI paths for snapshot/time-range/version-matrix. Real Provider and benchmark results must be reported separately and never simulated.
+
+## Current acceptance boundary
+
+The current branch supports **configuration persistence, pure local grouping/ranking, a bounded no-tools quick-screen executor, and an isolated transactional reservation ledger**. Neither quick screen nor ledger is connected to the operation DAG/Worker, and task authorization does not yet freeze its policy SHA. Therefore the product does not yet run the end-to-end four-level scheduler or enforce these budgets in live tasks. Passing mocks do not prove real Provider effectiveness or scheduling.
